        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl lg:text-4xl font-semibold text-white mb-12 text-center">
          Questions answered
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-0">
          {[
            {
              q: "How is this different from regular cloud storage?",
              a: "Cloud storage gives you a file path. IPFS pinning gives you a content address (CID). The CID is a cryptographic hash of your content. If the content changes, the CID changes. This makes it verifiable and portable across providers.",
            },
            {
              q: "Is my data permanent?",
              a: "CIDs are permanent hashes; they always point to the same content. But availability requires active pinning. If you stop paying or unpin, your content can become unavailable. We don't claim 'permanent' or 'forever' storage.",
            },
            {
              q: "What happens if I unpin something?",
              a: "Unpinned content may remain cached on gateways temporarily, but it's no longer guaranteed. For content you need to stay available, keep it pinned and your account active.",
            },
            {
              q: "Can I pay with crypto?",
              a: "Yes.",
            },
            {
              q: "How does pricing work?",
              a: "Storage at 10 USD/TB. Bandwidth priced upfront. No API call charges, no per-request fees.",
            },
            {
              q: "I'm using another pinning service. Can I migrate?",
              a: "Yes. Pin your existing CIDs directly with `pinner pin <cid>`. Your CIDs don't change when you switch providers.",
            },
            {
              q: "How is Sia different from IPFS-native pinning?",
              a: "Most pinning services store your files on centralized servers and make them available via IPFS. Pinner stores them on the Sia network, a distributed storage protocol with cryptographic storage proofs. Your content is verifiably stored across independent hosts.",
            },
            {
              q: "What if my pinned content goes offline?",
              a: "Your pins are copied across multiple independent Sia hosts. Individual hosts can go offline, but your content stays available from others as long as your account is active.",
            },
            {
              q: "Can I use Pinner with a CDN?",
              a: "Yes. Pin your content to get a CID, then use a CDN or gateway for fast delivery. Pinner handles storage and the CDN handles speed.",
            },
          ].map((faq, i) => (
            <div class="border-b border-home-text/10 last:border-0" key={i}>
              <details class="group">
                <summary class="w-full text-left py-5 flex items-center justify-between gap-4 cursor-pointer list-none">
